---
import NarrativeLayout from '../layouts/NarrativeLayout.astro';
import ThreeRetailerComparison from '../components/ThreeRetailerComparison.astro';
import ERDToggle from '../components/ERDToggle.astro';
import GTINHierarchy from '../components/GTINHierarchy.astro';
import EntityTable from '../components/EntityTable.astro';
---

<NarrativeLayout title="Product Master Data Model — Lailara LLC">

  <!-- Part 1: The Hook -->
  <section id="hook">
    <p class="orientation">Part 1 of 3 — The Problem</p>
    <h1>Walmart, Costco, and UNFI are not asking about the same product.</h1>
    <p>
      The same jar of Calabrian Chili Marinara maps to three different GTINs,
      at three different packaging levels, across three different item setup forms.
      A flat product spreadsheet cannot represent this. Most specialty food brands
      discover that gap during their first retailer rejection.
    </p>
    <ThreeRetailerComparison />
  </section>

  <!-- Part 2: The Proof -->
  <section id="proof">
    <p class="orientation">Part 2 of 3 — The Model</p>
    <h2>Here is the model that prevents this.</h2>
    <p>
      One SKU — CHP-0009 — walked from brand through each, inner, case, and pallet.
      A GTIN assigned at every level that carries one. The attribute set each
      retailer demands, mapped to the entity that owns it.
    </p>

    <GTINHierarchy />

    <p>
      The case row states the problem plainly: the shipped dimensions are NULL.
      Costco and UNFI both require them. The model below assigns responsibility for filling them —
      one entity per obligation, one source of truth per field.
    </p>

    <ERDToggle />

    <h3>Each entity carries an obligation</h3>
    <p class="section-subtitle">
      What every node in the diagram is accountable for — and what breaks without it.
    </p>

    <EntityTable />
  </section>

  <!-- Part 3: The Evidence -->
  <section id="evidence">
    <p class="orientation">Part 3 of 3 — The Evidence</p>
    <h2>This isn't a whiteboard diagram — it runs.</h2>
    <p>
      The schema below is drawn directly from the Cinderhaven Data Platform,
      which processes product data for 50 SKUs across 6 retailers and 3
      distributors. The model is live in Postgres. The contracts are published
      in dbt. The drift guard runs before every release.
    </p>
    <!-- MarginMath component goes here (U6) -->
    <!-- dbt docs link goes here (U6) -->
    <!-- Cross-links go here (U6) -->
  </section>

</NarrativeLayout>
